## Context

The author asked where the project stands against REQUIREMENTS.md, and no
document answered. DESIGN.md maps requirements to design sections, eighteen
tickets cite an R-number, and SYNC.md tracks upstream — but nothing joins them,
so the standing of the twenty-eight promises had to be reconstructed from
primary sources each time it was asked for. This ticket writes it down and
guards it.

The page carries a progress bar because that is what was asked for, and two
of them because one would lie: almost every promise is designed and almost
none is shipped, and a single bar averages those into a number describing
neither. `designed` is the chain's own state; `delivered` means the promise
holds on stock upstream, which is where the implementation lives.

## Relevant files

- `spec/README.md` — the page. Also `spec/`'s landing page on the forge.
- `bench/check_progress.py` — the guard, in `make check`.
- `tests/test_check_progress.py` — fixture-repository suite, every failure
  case a positive control.
- `bench/check_governance.py`, `bench/check_chain_dedup.py` — both have a
  completeness check over `spec/`, and both fired on the new document. One
  line each.

## What the guard checks

Coverage is read from `spec/REQUIREMENTS.md`, never from the page: a guard
that reads only the document under guard cannot tell an omission from an
absence. Every requirement gets exactly one row, under the sheet's own
section, quoting the sheet's own title. Every bar is recomputed from the rows,
so no status exists in one place alone. Every digit must be an address, on
`check_terminology.py`'s rule and for the same reason.

The promise cell is exempt from the digit rule (R9's title carries a page
count) and the exemption is only safe while the quotation is exact, so
exactness is checked rather than trusted.

## Verification

- [x] `make check` green: five guards, 97 tests
- [x] the guard fires on the page as first written — it caught two hand-counted
      bars and their headline, which is why the bars are computed now
- [x] both completeness checks fired on the new document before it was
      registered, confirming that channel works
- [x] the reading-order table was cut when `check_chain_dedup.py` showed it
      restated what `README.md` owns

## Exit criteria

- [x] `spec/README.md` states the standing of all twenty-eight requirements
- [x] the bars cannot drift from the rows
- [x] `make check` runs the guard
- [x] no design number, threshold or measurement is restated on the page
